TECH = [
# ---------------- Basic
("Basic", "What programming language and tools did you use, and why?",
 '"Thank you. I used Python, because it has mature libraries for every step. pandas and DuckDB handled the data, scikit-learn and scikit-survival or lifelines handled the models and survival metrics, and the xgboost library provided XGBoost. I used git for version control, and Word and LaTeX for the write-up."',
 "Matter-of-fact. Name the tools you actually used, nothing more.",
 '"I would need to check the exact version numbers, but they are pinned in requirements.txt in the repository."'),
("Basic", "In simple terms, what is machine learning?",
 '"Machine learning is a way of building a model by letting an algorithm learn patterns from examples, instead of writing the rules by hand. In my case the examples are ICU stays, the inputs are measurements from the first 24 hours, and the model learns how those measurements relate to survival time."',
 "Simple and friendly, as if explaining to a clinician.",
 'You will know this one. Keep it to two sentences and tie it to your data.'),
("Basic", "What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning? Which did you use?",
 '"Supervised learning learns from examples where the answer is known, such as whether and when a patient died. Unsupervised learning looks for structure without an answer, for example clustering. My study is supervised, because every patient has a known survival time or censoring time."',
 "Confident, short.",
 '"Mine is supervised; I did not use unsupervised methods, so I would not want to go into detail on them."'),
("Basic", "What are training and test sets?",
 '"The training set is the data the model learns from. The test set is kept aside and used once at the end to measure how well the model works on patients it has never seen. In my study the test set is the held-out group of NCD patients, 20% of all NCD patients, and every model is evaluated on exactly that group."',
 "Clear and precise. Use your own term, held-out group of NCD patients.",
 '"The key idea is that the test patients are never seen during training or tuning."'),
("Basic", "Why did you use an 80/20 split?",
 '"An 80/20 split is common in MIMIC-based prediction studies. With about 30,000 NCD patients, 20% gives roughly 6,100 test patients, which is enough for stable estimates of the C-index and narrow confidence intervals, while leaving most of the data for training."',
 "Reasoned. Show it was a choice with a reason, not a default.",
 '"It is a convention; I did not test other ratios, but with this sample size the test set is large enough for precise estimates."'),
("Basic", "What is overfitting, and how did you guard against it?",
 '"Overfitting is when a model learns the noise in the training data and performs worse on new data. I guarded against it in three ways: hyperparameters were tuned by 5-fold cross-validation on training data only, the tree models have limits such as depth and minimum leaf size, and the test set was used once at the end."',
 "Steady. This is a standard question; answer it like one.",
 '"The main safeguard is that the test set played no part in any choice I made."'),
("Basic", "What is underfitting?",
 '"Underfitting is the opposite: the model is too simple to capture the real pattern, so it performs poorly even on the training data. A linear Cox model could underfit if the true relationships are strongly non-linear, which is one reason I also used the random survival forest and XGBoost."',
 "Brief.",
 'Two sentences are enough here.'),
("Basic", "What is a feature or predictor, and how many did you use?",
 '"A predictor is an input variable the model uses. I used 69, all taken from the first 24 hours of the ICU stay: age and sex, the minimum, maximum and mean of seven vital signs and fourteen laboratory tests, the lowest Glasgow Coma Scale score, total urine output, and whether the patient received invasive ventilation or a vasopressor."',
 "Precise. Knowing your own numbers builds credibility.",
 '"There are 69; I can show the full list from the appendix."'),
("Basic", "What is a hyperparameter? Give an example from your work.",
 '"A hyperparameter is a setting chosen before training rather than learned from the data. Examples in my study are the number of trees and the minimum leaf size in the random survival forest, and the learning rate, tree depth and number of boosting rounds in XGBoost."',
 "Plain.",
 '"I would have to check the exact search ranges, but they are listed in the methodology chapter."'),
("Basic", "What is cross-validation?",
 '"Cross-validation splits the training data into k parts, here five. The model is trained on four parts and checked on the fifth, and this rotates five times. The average score is used to choose hyperparameters, so the test set is never touched during tuning."',
 "Clear, step by step.",
 '"In short, it is a way to tune the model without looking at the test set."'),
("Basic", "What data preprocessing did you do?",
 '"I selected adult first ICU stays, mapped diagnoses to NCD groups, and built the outcomes. For predictors, I took values from the first 24 hours, with a six-hour look-back for laboratory tests drawn in the emergency department. Implausible values outside ranges from the MIMIC Code Repository were treated as recording errors and removed. Fahrenheit temperatures were converted to Celsius. Then each variable was summarised as minimum, maximum and mean."',
 "Organised. Follow the order of the pipeline.",
 '"The full list of cleaning rules is in the code and the methodology chapter; I can point to the exact ranges."'),
("Basic", "How did you handle missing data?",
 '"For the Cox model and the random survival forest, I used median imputation, with medians calculated from training data only, plus a missing indicator for each predictor with gaps. XGBoost handles missing values itself by learning which branch to send them down. This follows evidence that median imputation performs about as well as complex methods when key predictors such as age are complete."',
 "Confident. Mention that medians came from training data only.",
 '"The reasoning and the supporting studies are in Section 3 on missing data; I chose a simple, documented method on purpose."'),
("Basic", "What is feature scaling, and did you need it?",
 '"Scaling puts variables on a similar range, for example by standardising them. Tree models such as the random survival forest and XGBoost do not need it, because they split on thresholds. For the Cox model, scaling helps the penalised fitting and makes coefficients comparable, so I standardised predictors using training data statistics."',
 "Brief. If you did not scale for Cox, say so honestly.",
 '"Tree models do not need scaling; for Cox I would need to check whether standardisation was applied before the penalty."'),
("Basic", "What is a random seed, and why does it matter?",
 '"A random seed fixes the starting point of random processes such as the data split, bootstrap samples and tree building, so that running the code again gives the same results. I used seed 42 throughout, set in one configuration file."',
 "Light. This is a quick question.",
 'Short answer; nothing to fear here.'),
("Basic", "What hardware did you use, and how long did training take?",
 '"I worked on a personal Windows computer. Extracting the predictors from the large chartevents and labevents tables was the slowest step, so I filtered them once and cached the result. Among the models, the random survival forest took longest to train; the Cox model was fastest."',
 "Factual and relaxed.",
 '"I did not record exact times for every model, but I can give approximate figures from the logs."'),
("Basic", "How did you manage your code and versions?",
 '"I used git, with a commit for each step of the pipeline. Patient data, processed datasets and trained models are excluded from the repository by the gitignore file, in line with the PhysioNet data use agreement. Only code, aggregate tables and figures are committed."',
 "Professional.",
 '"The repository history shows every change and when it was made."'),
("Basic", "Can another researcher reproduce your results?",
 '"Yes, provided they have PhysioNet access. MIMIC-IV v3.1 is freely available to credentialed researchers, a script checks every downloaded file against the official SHA256 checksums, and the pipeline runs in a fixed order with a fixed random seed. The README lists the commands."',
 "Confident. Reproducibility is a strength of your study.",
 '"Exact numbers may differ very slightly across library versions, which is why the versions are recorded."'),
("Basic", "What is a baseline model, and what was yours?",
 '"A baseline is a simple reference model that more complex models should beat. In my study the Cox proportional hazards model is the natural baseline, because it is the standard method for survival data. The general approach also acts as a baseline for the disease-specific and transfer learning approaches."',
 "Clear.",
 '"My main reference point was the Cox general model; I did not include a severity score such as SOFA as a separate baseline."'),
("Basic", "What is data leakage, and how did you avoid it?",
 '"Leakage is when information that would not be available at prediction time gets into training, which makes results look better than they are. I avoided it by using only data from the first 24 hours as predictors, never using diagnosis codes as predictors because they are assigned at discharge, keeping one stay per patient so no patient is in both sets, and computing imputation medians and tuning only on training data."',
 "Thorough. This is a favourite examiner question; take your time.",
 '"Those are the safeguards I built in; if the panel sees another path for leakage, I would like to check it."'),
("Basic", "What is class imbalance, and was it a problem in your study?",
 '"Class imbalance is when one outcome is much rarer than the other. About 25% of all patients and 22.8% of NCD patients died within a year, so it is moderate. Survival models also use the time information from censored patients, which softens the problem, and I used stratified splitting so the test set has the same death rate."',
 "Measured.",
 '"It was moderate, not severe, so I did not use resampling methods."'),
("Basic", "What is a decision tree?",
 '"A decision tree splits patients step by step on questions such as whether lactate is above a threshold, until it reaches groups with similar outcomes. On its own it is unstable, but it is the building block of the random survival forest and XGBoost."',
 "Simple.",
 'Two sentences are enough.'),
("Basic", "What is a random forest?",
 '"A random forest builds many decision trees, each on a bootstrap sample of patients and a random subset of predictors, and averages their predictions. The averaging reduces variance. The random survival forest applies this idea to censored survival data."',
 "Plain.",
 'Keep it short; the survival version is covered elsewhere.'),
("Basic", "What is gradient boosting, and what is XGBoost?",
 '"Gradient boosting builds trees one after another, with each new tree correcting the errors of the trees before it. XGBoost is an efficient implementation with regularisation and built-in handling of missing values. It also supports survival objectives, which is what I used."',
 "Clear.",
 '"The key difference from a random forest is that trees are built in sequence, not independently."'),
("Basic", "What is logistic regression, and why did your earlier analysis use it?",
 '"Logistic regression models the probability of a yes-or-no outcome as a function of the predictors. My earlier, now legacy, analysis predicted in-hospital death, so logistic regression was the natural linear baseline. In the survival study the Cox model takes that role."',
 "Brief, and make clear the legacy work is not the main analysis.",
 '"That analysis is superseded; the main results are the survival models."'),
("Basic", "What is the difference between accuracy and AUC?",
 '"Accuracy is the share of correct yes-or-no predictions at one threshold, and it can look good when one outcome is rare. AUC measures how well the model ranks patients who have the event above those who do not, across all thresholds. For survival I use the C-index, which is the survival version of that ranking idea, and time-dependent AUC."',
 "Clear and connected to your metrics.",
 '"The short answer is that AUC is about ranking, accuracy is about a single cut-off."'),
# ---------------- Intermediate
("Intermediate", "Why did you choose these three methods and not others?",
 '"Thank you. The three cover the main families. Cox is the standard statistical model for survival data and is interpretable. The random survival forest is a well-established machine learning method for censored data. XGBoost is the method that most often performs best in recent ICU prediction studies, and it supports survival objectives. Together they let me check whether the ranking of the three approaches depends on the method."',
 "Reasoned. Show the choice covers a range, not a random pick.",
 '"There are other options, such as DeepSurv; I list them as future work because the study\'s focus is the comparison of approaches, not of every method."'),
("Intermediate", "Why did you not use deep learning?",
 '"Two reasons. My predictors are tabular summaries, where tree-based methods usually perform as well as deep learning, and deep learning makes transfer learning and interpretation harder to do fairly across all three approaches. I note deep survival models such as DeepSurv as future work; a 2026 comparison found it competitive on MIMIC-IV."',
 "Calm. Not using a method is a valid design choice when explained.",
 '"It was a scope decision; I would be glad to extend the study in that direction."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you tune the hyperparameters?",
 '"I used randomised search with 5-fold cross-validation on the training data only, choosing the settings with the best average C-index. For transfer learning, the general model was refitted without each validation fold during tuning, so that no validation patient had influenced the general model being adapted."',
 "Precise. The refitting detail shows care; mention it.",
 '"The exact grids are in the appendix; I can read them out if the panel wishes."'),
("Intermediate", "How do you know you did not overfit to the test set?",
 '"The test set was used once, after every model and hyperparameter was fixed. All tuning used cross-validation inside the training data. I did not go back and change models after seeing test results."',
 "Firm and direct.",
 '"That discipline is the main protection; the commit history also shows the order in which things were done."'),
("Intermediate", "What is stratification, and why did you stratify your split?",
 '"Stratification keeps the share of an outcome the same in both parts of the split. I stratified by one-year death so the training data and the held-out group of NCD patients have the same death rate. Otherwise random chance could give a test set with unusually many or few deaths."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "Why does it matter that the split is at patient level?",
 '"If one patient had several ICU stays in different sets, the model could recognise that patient rather than learn general patterns. I kept only the first ICU stay per patient, so each patient appears once, and the split cannot leak a patient between training and test."',
 "Confident.",
 '"Keeping one stay per patient makes this automatic."'),
("Intermediate", "What is calibration, and why is it as important as discrimination?",
 '"Discrimination is whether the model ranks high-risk patients above low-risk ones. Calibration is whether the predicted probabilities match what actually happens: if the model says 30% risk of death within a year, about 30% of such patients should die. A model can rank well but give wrong absolute risks, which would mislead clinicians. So I report both."',
 "Engaged. This shows you understand clinical use.",
 '"Discrimination is about order; calibration is about the numbers being believable."'),
("Intermediate", "What is the Brier score?",
 '"The Brier score is the mean squared difference between predicted probability and what happened, so lower is better. For survival data it is calculated at a time point with weighting for censoring, and the integrated Brier score averages it over the follow-up period. It reflects both discrimination and calibration."',
 "Precise.",
 '"The key point: lower is better, and it captures calibration as well as ranking."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you calculate confidence intervals?",
 '"I used bootstrap resampling of the held-out group of NCD patients, 1,000 resamples, and took the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of each metric. The bootstrap does not assume a particular distribution for the C-index or the Brier score."',
 "Calm, technical.",
 '"If the panel prefers an analytic interval, I could add one, but the bootstrap is the usual choice for these metrics."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you test whether one approach is significantly better than another?",
 '"I bootstrapped the difference in C-index between paired approaches on the same test patients. Because every model predicts for the same patients, the comparison is paired. If the 95% interval of the difference excludes zero, the difference is statistically significant."',
 "Clear. Mention that the comparison is paired.",
 '"I used the paired bootstrap; I would need to check whether a formal test for comparing C-indices would give a different result."'),
("Intermediate", "What is the bias-variance trade-off?",
 '"Simple models have high bias, they miss real patterns, but low variance. Flexible models have low bias but can vary a lot with the training sample. Good models balance the two. The disease-specific approach is a good example: it may reduce bias for NCD patients, but with fewer patients it has higher variance. Transfer learning tries to get both."',
 "Thoughtful. Linking it to your approaches is a strong move.",
 '"In my study it appears as the trade-off between data relevance and data quantity."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you check the quality of the data?",
 '"I verified every downloaded file against the PhysioNet checksums, removed physiologically implausible values, checked missingness for all 69 predictors, and compared the cohort characteristics with published MIMIC-IV descriptions. The exploratory analysis, including Kaplan-Meier curves, was also a check that the outcomes behaved sensibly."',
 "Organised.",
 '"Those were the main checks; I did not do a formal audit against original charts, which is not possible with de-identified data."'),
("Intermediate", "What is a sensitivity analysis, and why did you do one?",
 '"A sensitivity analysis repeats the main analysis under a different reasonable assumption to see whether the conclusion holds. My main analysis defines NCD patients by principal diagnosis. The sensitivity analysis widens it to an NCD among the first three diagnoses, which gives 40,733 patients instead of 30,467. If the ranking holds under both, the conclusion does not depend on one definition."',
 "Confident.",
 '"The purpose is to show the result is not an artefact of one definition."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you choose your predictors?",
 '"I chose variables that are routinely measured in the first 24 hours of nearly every ICU stay and are used in severity scores and in earlier MIMIC studies: vital signs, common laboratory tests, the Glasgow Coma Scale, urine output, ventilation and vasopressor use, plus age and sex. Item codes followed the MIMIC Code Repository."',
 "Reasoned.",
 '"The selection was based on clinical relevance and availability, not on testing every possible variable."'),
("Intermediate", "Why did you not do automated feature selection?",
 '"The number of predictors, 69, is small relative to the number of patients, so there is little risk of having too many. Tree methods handle irrelevant predictors well, and the Cox model can be penalised. Also, using the same predictor set for all approaches keeps the comparison fair."',
 "Calm.",
 '"Feature selection could be added, but it would have to be repeated identically for every approach."'),
("Intermediate", "How interpretable are your models?",
 '"The Cox model is directly interpretable through hazard ratios. The random survival forest and XGBoost are less transparent, but variable importance or SHAP values can show which predictors drive predictions. Since the aim was to compare approaches, interpretability was secondary, but I report the most important predictors."',
 "Honest.",
 '"I prioritised comparison over explanation; explanation is an obvious next step."'),
("Intermediate", "Did you explain individual predictions, for example with SHAP?",
 'If you did: "Yes. I used SHAP values for the XGBoost models, which show how much each predictor pushed a patient\'s risk up or down." If you did not: "Not in this study. SHAP would be a natural addition for clinical use, and earlier MIMIC-IV studies, such as Gu and Lu on cancer patients, used it."',
 "Straightforward. Do not claim analysis you did not do.",
 '"I did not include it in this version; I can see how it would strengthen the clinical side."'),
("Intermediate", "What is regularisation, and where did you use it?",
 '"Regularisation adds a penalty for complexity to stop overfitting. In XGBoost it penalises large leaf weights. For transfer learning with Cox, a penalty keeps the adapted model close to the general model unless the NCD data clearly supports a change."',
 "Technical but clear.",
 '"The main use is in XGBoost and in the transfer learning adjustment."'),
("Intermediate", "What is external validation, and why did you not do it?",
 '"External validation tests a model on data from a different source, such as another hospital. I did not do it because it was outside the scope and time of an MSc study, and access to a second database takes time. Because all approaches face the same limitation, the comparison between them is still fair. I list eICU validation as future work."',
 "Honest, not apologetic.",
 '"It is the most important next step, and I state that clearly in the limitations."'),
("Intermediate", "What is the difference between internal and external validation?",
 '"Internal validation uses data from the same source, such as a held-out split or cross-validation; mine is internal. External validation uses data from another hospital, region or time period, which tests transportability."',
 "Brief.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How would your model be used in a hospital?",
 '"At the end of the first 24 hours in the ICU, the system would pull the predictors from the electronic record, compute the survival curve, and show estimated survival at 28 days, 90 days and one year. It would support, not replace, clinical judgement. Before any use it would need local validation and approval."',
 "Practical and cautious.",
 '"Deployment was outside my scope, so this is how I would expect it to work rather than something I tested."'),
("Intermediate", "What are the computational costs of each method?",
 '"The Cox model is cheapest to train and predict. XGBoost is fast because of its efficient implementation. The random survival forest is the most expensive in time and memory, and its saved models are large. For prediction at the bedside, all three are fast enough."',
 "Factual.",
 '"I can give the approximate training times from my logs."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you handle outliers and implausible values?",
 '"I used plausible ranges from the MIMIC Code Repository, for example heart rate between 0 and 300, and removed values outside them as recording errors. Genuine extreme values within the range were kept, because in the ICU they are often real and informative."',
 "Clear.",
 '"The exact ranges are in features.py and the appendix."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you handle categorical variables?",
 '"There are few. Sex was coded as 0 or 1, and ventilation and vasopressor use are binary indicators. The missing indicators are also binary. The rest are continuous."',
 "Brief.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "Can your results show that a predictor causes death?",
 '"No. This is a prediction study. A predictor can be useful for prediction without causing the outcome. For example, vasopressor use predicts death because it marks how sick a patient is, not because the drug causes death. Causal questions need a different design."',
 "Firm and clear.",
 '"My claims are about prediction only."'),
("Intermediate", "What is algorithmic bias, and did you check performance across groups?",
 '"Bias here means a model performing worse for some groups. My main subgroup check is across the four NCD groups, because that is my research question. Checking performance by sex and by race or ethnicity would also be good practice; Cummins and colleagues did this for stroke on MIMIC-IV."',
 "Responsible. If you did not check sex or race, say so.",
 '"I reported subgroup results by NCD group; I did not report them by race, and I would add that in further work."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you make sure your code was correct?",
 '"I built the pipeline in small steps with checks at each one: checksum verification of the raw files, counts printed at each cohort step and compared with expectations, checks that each stay appears once, and Kaplan-Meier curves to confirm the outcomes made sense. My supervisor also reviewed the intermediate results."',
 "Calm.",
 '"No code is guaranteed bug-free, but each step had a check I could verify."'),
("Intermediate", "What is the difference between statistical and clinical significance?",
 '"A difference can be statistically significant because the sample is large, yet too small to matter for patients. With about 6,100 test patients, a C-index difference of 0.005 might be significant but not clinically useful. I interpret differences in terms of both."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"In short: significant does not always mean meaningful."'),
("Intermediate", "Why does a high AUC or C-index not guarantee a useful model?",
 '"Because it only measures ranking. A model can rank well but be badly calibrated, or be good overall but poor for a subgroup, or be good on old data but not on new patients. That is why I also report calibration, the Brier score and per-group results."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"Ranking is necessary but not sufficient."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you document your pipeline?",
 '"Each script starts with a description of what it does, its inputs and outputs. The README gives the order to run them. Results tables carry the definition and sample size in their headers, and the thesis methodology chapter describes every step."',
 "Brief.",
 'Short answer.'),
# ---------------- Advanced
("Advanced", "What are the main threats to the validity of your study?",
 '"For internal validity, the main threats are missing data and how diagnoses were coded. For construct validity, the NCD definition depends on the principal diagnosis code, which may not always be the reason for ICU admission. For external validity, the single US centre. I addressed these with a documented missing-data method, a sensitivity analysis on the NCD definition, and full reporting of the cohort."',
 "Structured. Use the validity types as headings in your head.",
 '"Those are the threats I identified; I am open to others the panel sees."'),
("Advanced", "How would dataset shift affect your model over time?",
 '"MIMIC-IV covers admissions from about 2008 to 2019. Treatments, coding practice and patient mix change, so a model trained then may lose accuracy later. Coding changed from ICD-9 to ICD-10 during the period, which I handled by mapping both. In use, the model would need monitoring and periodic recalibration."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"I did not test temporal validation, for example training on early years and testing on later years, but it would be a useful check."'),
("Advanced", "How would you monitor a deployed model?",
 '"I would track discrimination and calibration on new patients over time, watch for changes in the distribution of predictors, and set thresholds for recalibration or retraining. Performance should also be tracked separately for each NCD group."',
 "Practical.",
 '"Monitoring was outside my scope, but that is the standard approach I would follow."'),
("Advanced", "What privacy risks remain with de-identified data, and how did you respect them?",
 '"De-identified data can still be re-identified if combined with other sources, and models can memorise individual records. That is why I signed the data use agreement, kept data and models local, never tried to identify anyone, and shared only aggregate results with no small cell counts."',
 "Serious.",
 '"I followed the PhysioNet rules strictly; beyond that I would defer to the data protection office."'),
("Advanced", "Why did you not publish your trained models?",
 '"Trained models, especially random forests, can store information about individual training patients, and the PhysioNet agreement does not allow redistributing the data. So the models stay local. Another credentialed researcher can retrain them from the code."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "Did you do a sample size calculation?",
 '"I did not do a formal calculation before the study because the sample was fixed by the database. With 30,467 NCD patients and about 6,900 deaths within a year, the number of events per predictor is far above common minimums for 69 predictors. The smallest NCD groups are where sample size limits precision, which I report."',
 "Honest and numerate.",
 '"A formal calculation using current guidance for prediction models would be a good addition; I am confident the overall sample is adequate."'),
("Advanced", "You compare many models. How do you deal with multiple comparisons?",
 '"The main comparison is three approaches within each method, which is a small number of planned comparisons. I focus on confidence intervals and the consistency of the ranking across methods rather than single p-values. A finding that appears for all three methods is much stronger than one significant difference."',
 "Calm, statistical.",
 '"I did not apply a formal correction; I would be cautious about any single borderline result."'),
("Advanced", "What does the no free lunch theorem mean for your study?",
 '"It says no single algorithm is best for every problem. That is why I used three different methods, and why my conclusions are about this population and data, not a universal ranking."',
 "Brief, thoughtful.",
 '"The practical lesson is to test several methods, which I did."'),
("Advanced", "How sensitive are your results to the random seed?",
 'If you checked: "I repeated the split and training with different seeds and the ranking held." If not: "The test set is large, so bootstrap intervals already reflect sampling uncertainty. Repeating with several seeds would confirm stability, and I would add it."',
 "Honest.",
 '"I did not run multiple seeds; the confidence intervals are my main measure of uncertainty."'),
("Advanced", "What if the missing data are not missing at random?",
 '"In the ICU they often are not. Lactate or bilirubin are ordered when a clinician is worried, so missingness itself carries information. The missing indicators let the Cox model and random survival forest use that information, and XGBoost learns it directly. Sisk and colleagues found missing indicators helped in many cases, though they can harm if missingness depends on the outcome."',
 "Engaged. This is a strong point for you.",
 '"I cannot fully rule out bias from informative missingness, but the indicators reduce it."'),
("Advanced", "Why median imputation instead of multiple imputation?",
 '"Multiple imputation is more principled for inference, but for prediction it is hard to apply at deployment, because the outcome cannot be used to impute a new patient. Berkelmans and colleagues found median imputation non-inferior for prediction when key predictors are complete. It is also simple to reproduce."',
 "Confident.",
 '"It was a pragmatic choice; a comparison with multiple imputation could be a sensitivity analysis."'),
("Advanced", "Which reporting guideline did you follow?",
 '"The TRIPOD guidance for prediction model studies, and its update for machine learning, TRIPOD+AI, guided what I report: data source, participants, outcome, predictors, missing data, model development and performance measures."',
 "Confident if true. Only claim this if you checked the checklist.",
 '"I followed its main items; I can add a completed checklist as an appendix."'),
("Advanced", "Would your model be regulated as a medical device?",
 '"If used to inform individual patient care, yes, in many jurisdictions software that makes clinical predictions is regulated as a medical device. My study is research, not a product, so it would need validation, clinical evaluation and approval before use."',
 "Measured.",
 '"I am not an expert on regulation; I would expect it to need formal approval."'),
("Advanced", "What is automation bias, and how could it affect your model's use?",
 '"Automation bias is when people trust a computer output too much and override their own judgement. A confident survival estimate could lead a team to give up on a patient too early. The model should be presented as one input among many, with its uncertainty shown."',
 "Serious and reflective.",
 '"It is a real risk, and it is why I stress decision support, not decision making."'),
("Advanced", "How would you explain one patient's prediction to a clinician?",
 '"I would show the predicted survival curve, with estimates at 28 days, 90 days and one year, and the main factors that raised or lowered the risk, for example a high lactate or low Glasgow Coma Scale. For the Cox model these come from hazard ratios; for XGBoost from SHAP values."',
 "Practical.",
 '"I did not build that interface, but that is how I would present it."'),
("Advanced", "How would you update the model when new data arrive?",
 '"Two options: recalibrate the existing model, which is cheap and fixes drift in average risk, or retrain on newer data. Interestingly, transfer learning is itself a way to update: you can take an existing model and adapt it to a new hospital with its own data."',
 "Engaged.",
 '"Updating strategy was not part of my study, but transfer learning gives a natural route."'),
("Advanced", "If a model is better on average but worse for one subgroup, which would you choose?",
 '"It depends on the use. If the model is meant for NCD patients, performance in that group is what counts, which is the whole point of my study. More generally, I would report both and avoid a model that is clearly worse for a group it will be used on. Pfohl and colleagues showed this trade-off is real."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"There is no single answer; I would make the trade-off visible to decision makers."'),
("Advanced", "Is your study about prediction or explanation?",
 '"Prediction. The aim is to estimate survival accurately for new patients and compare ways of building such models. I do not claim any predictor causes death, and hazard ratios from the Cox model should not be read as causal effects."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "What assumptions do your evaluation metrics make?",
 '"The usual estimates of the C-index and time-dependent AUC assume censoring is independent of the outcome given the predictors. The censoring-weighted Brier score assumes the censoring model is correct. In my data most censoring is administrative at 365 days, which fits these assumptions well."',
 "Precise.",
 '"I would need to check the exact assumptions of each estimator, but the key one is independent censoring."'),
("Advanced", "Is a C-index of 0.80 really better than 0.78 in practice?",
 '"Not necessarily. A small difference in ranking may not change any decision. To judge practical value I look at calibration and the size of the difference with its confidence interval. Decision curve analysis would show whether the difference changes net benefit at realistic thresholds."',
 "Balanced.",
 '"A small gain in C-index alone would not convince me to change models."'),
("Advanced", "What is decision curve analysis, and why did you not do it?",
 '"Decision curve analysis estimates the net benefit of using a model to make decisions across a range of risk thresholds, compared with treating everyone or no one. I did not include it because my focus was comparing approaches on standard performance measures, and there is no single agreed decision for one-year ICU survival. It would be a good addition."',
 "Honest.",
 '"I know the idea but did not apply it; I would add it in follow-up work."'),
("Advanced", "How would your approach scale to many hospitals?",
 '"Transfer learning suits this: one general model trained on a large pooled dataset, then adapted to each hospital or disease group. Where data cannot be shared, federated learning could train a general model across hospitals without moving patient records."',
 "Forward-looking.",
 '"That is beyond my study, but the design extends naturally."'),
("Advanced", "If your best model is a black box, is it acceptable for clinical use?",
 '"It can be, if it is well validated, well calibrated, and its outputs are explained with methods such as SHAP. But if a transparent model like Cox performs about as well, I would prefer it, because clinicians can check its reasoning."',
 "Balanced.",
 '"I would favour the simpler model when performance is similar."'),
("Advanced", "What result would have made you reject your expectations?",
 '"I expected transfer learning to perform at least as well as the other two approaches. If the disease-specific or general approach had clearly beaten it across all three methods, that would contradict my expectation, and I would report it as it is."',
 "Honest. Show you are open to being wrong.",
 '"A negative result is still a result; I would report it in the same way."'),
("Advanced", "What is the weakest point of your methodology, and how do you defend it?",
 '"I would say the reliance on one database from one centre. I defend it in two ways. All approaches are compared on the same data, so the comparison between them is fair even if absolute performance may differ elsewhere. And the design is fully documented, so it can be repeated on other data."',
 "Candid and confident. Choosing your own weakness shows maturity.",
 '"Every study has a weakest point; I would rather name it myself."'),
]
